<!-- Section 1: sec-intro -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Pythagorean Triples Calculator</h2>
  <p>Check whole-number triples and generate new ones with Euclid's formula.</p>
  <p>A Pythagorean triple is a set of three positive whole numbers that can serve as the sides of a right triangle. The two shorter numbers are the legs, the largest number is the hypotenuse, and the three values satisfy the Pythagorean theorem.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The Pythagorean Triples Calculator does two jobs. In check mode, you enter three whole numbers and it tests whether they form a valid triple. In generate mode, you enter two integers, <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} />, and it builds a new triple with Euclid's formula. Checking and generating are different tasks, and this page explains both, step by step.</p>
  <p>The best-known example is <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} />. Squaring and adding the legs gives <span set:html={rich('[[3^2+4^2=9+16=25]]')} />, which equals <span set:html={rich('[[5^2]]')} />. Because the two sides match exactly, <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 4, 5)]]')} /> is a valid triple. A primitive triple is one whose three numbers share no common factor greater than <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} />; <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} /> have nothing in common, so <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 4, 5)]]')} /> is primitive. The sections below show how to check any three numbers, how to generate triples of any size, and how to tell primitive triples from scaled copies.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: sec-calculate -->
<section id="sec-calculate" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Check or Generate Pythagorean Triples With the Calculator</h2>
  <p>Two modes, one page: check a set of numbers, or build a new triple.</p>
  <h3>Mode 1: Check a Pythagorean Triple</h3>
  <p>Enter the three side lengths as <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, with <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> as the largest value. The calculator squares <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, adds the results, squares <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, and compares the two sides of the equation.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>When both sides are equal, the three numbers form a valid Pythagorean triple, and the result says so. When they differ, the numbers do not form a triple, and the result shows the two unequal totals so you can see how far off they are. All three inputs must be positive whole numbers, and all three must use the same unit.</p>
  <h3>Mode 2: Generate Pythagorean Triples</h3>
  <p>Enter two positive integers, <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} />, with <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> greater than <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} />. The calculator applies Euclid's formula to produce three whole-number side lengths.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a=m^2-n^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b=2mn]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c=m^2+n^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The result is always a valid triple, because Euclid's formula is built to satisfy the Pythagorean theorem. The two legs may appear in either order, but <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is always the hypotenuse. To use the calculator, pick a mode, enter valid numbers, click Calculate, and read the result with its step-by-step working. Click Reset to clear the fields and start again.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: sec-what -->
<section id="sec-what" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a Pythagorean Triple?</h2>
  <p>Three positive whole numbers that fit the Pythagorean theorem exactly.</p>
  <p>A Pythagorean triple is a set of three positive integers, written <span set:html={rich('[[(a, b, c)]]')} />, that satisfy the equation <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} />. Here <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are the legs, the two sides that meet at the right angle, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse, the side opposite the right angle and always the longest of the three.</p>
  <p>The equation must hold exactly. A near miss is not a triple: if the sum of the squared legs differs from the squared hypotenuse by even one unit, the three numbers fail the test. That strict equality is what makes a triple useful in building and carpentry, where a <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} /> check marks a square corner with no measuring error.</p>
  <p>All three numbers must be whole and positive. A right triangle with decimal side lengths, such as legs <span set:html={rich('[[1.5]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[2]]')} /> with hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[2.5]]')} />, satisfies the Pythagorean theorem but is not a Pythagorean triple, because the definition requires integers. Likewise, a triangle with an irrational side, such as legs <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} /> with hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{2}]]')} />, is a right triangle but not a triple. The word triple is reserved for positive integer solutions only.</p>
  <p>The triple <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 4, 5)]]')} /> is the natural first example. Its legs are <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} />, its hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[9+16=25]]')} /> confirms the equation. The order of the legs does not matter: <span set:html={rich('[[(4, 3, 5)]]')} /> describes the same triangle. What matters is that the two smaller numbers are the legs and the largest is the hypotenuse.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('pythagorean-triples-squares.svg')} alt="Squares drawn on the sides 3, 4, and 5 with areas 9, 16, and 25, showing 9 plus 16 equals 25" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Squares on the sides 3, 4, and 5 have areas 9, 16, and 25. Since 9 + 16 = 25, the numbers 3, 4, and 5 form a Pythagorean triple.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: sec-pythagorean -->
<section id="sec-pythagorean" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Pythagorean Theorem</h2>
  <p>The theorem behind every triple: the squares of the legs add to the square of the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>The Pythagorean theorem states that in any right triangle, the square of the hypotenuse equals the sum of the squares of the other two sides. With legs <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, the theorem reads as follows.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>One clear way to see it is through areas. Draw a square on each side of the triangle. The square on leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> has area <span set:html={rich('[[a^2]]')} />, the square on leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> has area <span set:html={rich('[[b^2]]')} />, and the square on the hypotenuse has area <span set:html={rich('[[c^2]]')} />. The theorem says the first two areas add up to the third. For the <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} /> triangle, the squares have areas <span set:html={rich('[[9]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[16]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[25]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[9+16=25]]')} /> shows the balance directly.</p>
  <p>That area picture also explains why <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} /> is a valid right triangle. Its side lengths satisfy the area balance exactly, so the angle between the legs must be a right angle. Every Pythagorean triple satisfies the theorem, because a triple is defined by that equation. The reverse is not true: plenty of right triangles have side lengths that are not whole numbers, so they satisfy the theorem without being triples. Use the <a href="/pythagorean-theorem-calculator/">Pythagorean theorem calculator</a> when a triangle has decimal or unknown sides instead of whole-number triples.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('pythagorean-triples-theorem.svg')} alt="Right triangle ABC with legs a equals 3 and b equals 4, hypotenuse c equals 5, a right-angle marker, and the formula a squared plus b squared equals c squared" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Right triangle ABC with legs a = 3 and b = 4 and hypotenuse c = 5. The right-angle marker sits at C, and 3 squared plus 4 squared equals 5 squared.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: sec-check -->
<section id="sec-check" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Three Numbers Form a Pythagorean Triple</h2>
  <p>Eight short steps turn any three whole numbers into a clear yes or no.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the largest of the three values.</li>
    <li>Assign the largest value to <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li>Assign the other two values to <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, the legs.</li>
    <li>Square <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Square <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Add the two squares.</li>
    <li>Square <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Compare the two results: equal means a valid triple, unequal means not a triple.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Here is a valid example. Check <span set:html={rich('[[7]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[24]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[25]]')} />: the largest value is <span set:html={rich('[[25]]')} />, so it is the hypotenuse. Square the legs and add.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[7^2+24^2=25^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[49+576=625]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Since <span set:html={rich('[[625=625]]')} />, the two sides match, so <span set:html={rich('[[(7, 24, 25)]]')} /> is a valid Pythagorean triple. It is also primitive, because <span set:html={rich('[[7]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[24]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[25]]')} /> share no common factor greater than <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Now an invalid example. Check <span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[6]]')} />: the largest value is <span set:html={rich('[[6]]')} />, so it is the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[4^2+5^2 \\neq 6^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[16+25=41]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[6^2=36]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Since <span set:html={rich('[[41]]')} /> does not equal <span set:html={rich('[[36]]')} />, the values do not form a Pythagorean triple. The same eight steps work for any positive integer inputs, large or small, so exact verification never depends on memory or guesswork.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('pythagorean-triples-check-flow.svg')} alt="Step-by-step flow for checking 7, 24, 25: square the legs to 49 and 576, add to 625, square the hypotenuse to 625, and confirm equality" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Checking 7, 24, 25 step by step: 49 + 576 = 625 and 25 squared = 625, so the two sides match and the triple is valid.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: sec-euclid -->
<section id="sec-euclid" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Generate Pythagorean Triples Using Euclid's Formula</h2>
  <p>Two small integers in, a full triple out: that is Euclid's formula.</p>
  <p>Euclid's formula builds a Pythagorean triple from two positive integers, <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} />, with <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> greater than <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} />. The parameter <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> is the larger integer and <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} /> is the smaller one, and both must be whole numbers. The three formulas produce the two legs and the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a=m^2-n^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b=2mn]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c=m^2+n^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The condition <span set:html={rich('[[m>n>0]]')} /> matters. If <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> equals <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} />, the first leg becomes zero, which is not a triangle side. If <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> is smaller than <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} />, the first leg becomes negative, which is also impossible. Decimal values of <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} /> are not valid here either, because the formula is designed for integer parameters that produce integer sides.</p>
  <p>Try <span set:html={rich('[[m=2]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n=1]]')} />. The first leg is <span set:html={rich('[[a=2^2-1^2=4-1=3]]')} />, the second leg is <span set:html={rich('[[b=2(2)(1)=4]]')} />, and the hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[c=2^2+1^2=4+1=5]]')} />. The result is the triple <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 4, 5)]]')} />. Notice that the generated legs can appear in either order: the formula may return the larger leg first or second, but <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is always the hypotenuse. The next section proves why these formulas always satisfy the Pythagorean theorem.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('pythagorean-triples-euclid-flow.svg')} alt="Euclid&#x27;s formula flowchart with m equals 3 and n equals 2 producing 5, 12, and 13" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Euclid's formula with m = 3 and n = 2: m squared minus n squared is 5, 2mn is 12, and m squared plus n squared is 13, giving the triple 5, 12, 13.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: sec-proof -->
<section id="sec-proof" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Mathematical Proof of Euclid's Formula</h2>
  <p>A short algebra proof shows why Euclid's formula can never fail.</p>
  <p>Start with the two leg formulas, <span set:html={rich('[[a=m^2-n^2]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=2mn]]')} />, and test whether <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2]]')} /> equals <span set:html={rich('[[c^2]]')} /> when <span set:html={rich('[[c=m^2+n^2]]')} />. Substitute the formulas into the left side of the Pythagorean equation.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2+b^2=(m^2-n^2)^2+(2mn)^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Expand both squares. The first square expands to <span set:html={rich('[[m^4-2m^2n^2+n^4]]')} /> and the second to <span set:html={rich('[[4m^2n^2]]')} />, so the sum becomes the following expression.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[=m^4-2m^2n^2+n^4+4m^2n^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Combine the middle terms: <span set:html={rich('[[-2m^2n^2+4m^2n^2]]')} /> is <span set:html={rich('[[+2m^2n^2]]')} />. The expression is now a perfect square in disguise.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[=m^4+2m^2n^2+n^4]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Factor it. The result is the square of <span set:html={rich('[[m^2+n^2]]')} />, which is exactly <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[=(m^2+n^2)^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Since <span set:html={rich('[[c=m^2+n^2]]')} />, the right side is <span set:html={rich('[[c^2]]')} />, and the proof is complete.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The key step is the middle terms cancelling partway: the <span set:html={rich('[[-2m^2n^2]]')} /> from the first square and the <span set:html={rich('[[+4m^2n^2]]')} /> from the second leave exactly the <span set:html={rich('[[+2m^2n^2]]')} /> needed to complete the square. Because this works for any integers <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} /> with <span set:html={rich('[[m>n>0]]')} />, every pair generates a genuine Pythagorean triple. The proof says nothing about primitiveness, though: whether the result is primitive depends on the extra conditions in the sections below.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: sec-primitive -->
<section id="sec-primitive" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a Primitive Pythagorean Triple?</h2>
  <p>A primitive triple is a triple in its simplest form, with no shared factor.</p>
  <p>A primitive Pythagorean triple is one whose three side lengths have no common positive integer factor greater than <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} />. In symbols, the greatest common divisor of the three sides is <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\gcd(a,b,c)=1]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The triple <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 4, 5)]]')} /> is primitive because <span set:html={rich('[[\\gcd(3,4,5)=1]]')} />: no integer larger than <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} /> divides all three numbers. The same holds for <span set:html={rich('[[(5, 12, 13)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[(8, 15, 17)]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[(7, 24, 25)]]')} />. Each of these is a base triple that cannot be reduced to a smaller whole-number triple by division.</p>
  <p>Primitive does not mean the triple is the smallest possible in every sense. It means only that the three values share no common factor other than <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} />. A large primitive triple such as <span set:html={rich('[[(20, 21, 29)]]')} /> is still primitive, because <span set:html={rich('[[20]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[21]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[29]]')} /> have no common divisor. The table below compares the divisors of a primitive triple and a scaled copy.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Triple</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Common divisors</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Primitive?</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[(3, 4, 5)]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[(5, 12, 13)]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[(8, 15, 17)]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[(6, 8, 10)]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[1, 2]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">No</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[(9, 12, 15)]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[1, 3]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">No</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
