    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The 3-4-5 triangle is primitive, while 6-8-10 is twice as large in every side: gcd(3, 4, 5) = 1, but gcd(6, 8, 10) = 2.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 9: sec-nonprimitive -->
<section id="sec-nonprimitive" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a Non-Primitive Pythagorean Triple?</h2>
  <p>A non-primitive triple is a scaled copy of a primitive one.</p>
  <p>A non-primitive Pythagorean triple is a triple whose three values share a common integer factor greater than <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} />. It is still a valid triple, because it satisfies <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} /> exactly. It is simply not in simplest form.</p>
  <p>Start with the primitive triple <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 4, 5)]]')} /> and multiply every side by <span set:html={rich('[[2]]')} />. The result is <span set:html={rich('[[(6, 8, 10)]]')} />, and the check still works: <span set:html={rich('[[36+64=100]]')} />. Multiply by <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} /> instead and the result is <span set:html={rich('[[(9, 12, 15)]]')} />, with <span set:html={rich('[[81+144=225]]')} />. Both scaled triples are valid, and neither is primitive, because the first shares the factor <span set:html={rich('[[2]]')} /> and the second shares the factor <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Finding the common factor is simple: take the greatest common divisor of the three sides. For <span set:html={rich('[[(6, 8, 10)]]')} /> it is <span set:html={rich('[[2]]')} />; for <span set:html={rich('[[(9, 12, 15)]]')} /> it is <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} />. Dividing every side by that factor recovers the primitive triple underneath. So <span set:html={rich('[[(6, 8, 10)]]')} /> divided by <span set:html={rich('[[2]]')} /> returns <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 4, 5)]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[(9, 12, 15)]]')} /> divided by <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} /> returns <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 4, 5)]]')} /> as well. Every non-primitive triple reduces to exactly one primitive triple this way.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: sec-conditions -->
<section id="sec-conditions" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Conditions for Generating Primitive Pythagorean Triples</h2>
  <p>Euclid's formula makes a primitive triple only when both parameter conditions hold.</p>
  <p>Two conditions on <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} /> decide whether Euclid's formula produces a primitive triple. Both must hold at the same time, and neither alone is enough.</p>
  <h3>Condition 1: Coprime parameters</h3>
  <p>The parameters must be coprime, meaning they share no common factor greater than <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\gcd(m,n)=1]]]')} /></p>
  <p>When <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} /> share a factor, that factor leaks into all three generated sides. For example, <span set:html={rich('[[m=4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n=2]]')} /> share the factor <span set:html={rich('[[2]]')} />, and the formula produces <span set:html={rich('[[(12, 16, 20)]]')} />, whose sides all share the factor <span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} />. The result is non-primitive.</p>
  <h3>Condition 2: Opposite parity</h3>
  <p>One parameter must be even and the other odd. When both parameters are odd, the generated legs and hypotenuse all come out even, so the triple shares the factor <span set:html={rich('[[2]]')} /> and cannot be primitive. For example, <span set:html={rich('[[m=3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n=1]]')} /> are coprime but both odd, and the formula produces <span set:html={rich('[[(8, 6, 10)]]')} />, which is the non-primitive triple <span set:html={rich('[[(6, 8, 10)]]')} /> with the legs in a different order.</p>
  <p>Three parameter pairs show how the conditions work together. The pair <span set:html={rich('[[(2, 1)]]')} /> is coprime with opposite parity and gives the primitive triple <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 4, 5)]]')} />. The pair <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 2)]]')} /> is coprime with opposite parity and gives the primitive triple <span set:html={rich('[[(5, 12, 13)]]')} />. The pair <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 1)]]')} /> is coprime but both odd, so it gives the non-primitive triple <span set:html={rich('[[(6, 8, 10)]]')} />. Coprimality without opposite parity is not enough, and opposite parity without coprimality is not enough either.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('pythagorean-triples-conditions.svg')} alt="Two parameter examples: m equals 3 and n equals 2 give primitive 5-12-13, while m equals 3 and n equals 1 give non-primitive 6-8-10" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Parameters m = 3, n = 2 are coprime with opposite parity and give primitive 5-12-13. Parameters m = 3, n = 1 are both odd and give non-primitive 6-8-10.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: sec-difference -->
<section id="sec-difference" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Difference Between Primitive and Non-Primitive Triples</h2>
  <p>Same theorem, different form: one table sums up the whole distinction.</p>
  <p>Both kinds of triple satisfy the Pythagorean theorem exactly, so both describe genuine right triangles. The difference is only about shared factors and how the triple was produced. A primitive triple is a base pattern; a non-primitive triple is usually a multiple of one.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Feature</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Primitive</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Non-primitive</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Common divisor</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Only <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Greater than <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} /></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Side lengths</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Cannot be reduced to a smaller integer triple</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Can be divided by a common factor</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Example</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[3, 4, 5]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[6, 8, 10]]')} /></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Generation</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Coprime <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} /> with opposite parity</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Other parameter combinations or integer multiples</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Relationship</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Base integer triple</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Often a multiple of a primitive triple</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: sec-scaling -->
<section id="sec-scaling" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Generate More Triples by Scaling Existing Ones</h2>
  <p>Multiply every side by the same whole number and the triple stays valid.</p>
  <p>For any positive integer <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, multiplying every side of a Pythagorean triple <span set:html={rich('[[(a, b, c)]]')} /> by <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> gives another Pythagorean triple, <span set:html={rich('[[(ka, kb, kc)]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{(ka, kb, kc)}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The reason is simple algebra. Squaring the scaled legs gives <span set:html={rich('[[(ka)^2+(kb)^2=k^2a^2+k^2b^2]]')} />, and factoring out <span set:html={rich('[[k^2]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[k^2(a^2+b^2)]]')} />. Since the original triple satisfies <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} />, the scaled sum equals <span set:html={rich('[[k^2c^2]]')} />, which is <span set:html={rich('[[(kc)^2]]')} />. The scaled triple therefore satisfies the theorem exactly.</p>
  <p>Scaling <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 4, 5)]]')} /> by <span set:html={rich('[[2]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[(6, 8, 10)]]')} />, and scaling by <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[(9, 12, 15)]]')} />. Scaling <span set:html={rich('[[(5, 12, 13)]]')} /> by <span set:html={rich('[[2]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[(10, 24, 26)]]')} />, and scaling <span set:html={rich('[[(8, 15, 17)]]')} /> by <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[(24, 45, 51)]]')} />. Each result is a valid triple, and each is non-primitive, because the multiplier becomes a common factor of all three sides.</p>
  <p>An integer multiplier preserves whole-number side lengths, which is why scaling is a reliable way to build new triples. An arbitrary real multiplier, such as <span set:html={rich('[[1.5]]')} />, keeps the triangle a right triangle with the same shape, but the sides may stop being whole numbers: <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 4, 5)]]')} /> scaled by <span set:html={rich('[[1.5]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[(4.5, 6, 7.5)]]')} />, which is a right triangle but not a Pythagorean triple. Note that the calculator itself has no separate multiplier field; scaling is a manual calculation you do with the triples it checks or generates.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('pythagorean-triples-scaling.svg')} alt="The triple 3-4-5 multiplied by 2, 3, and 4 to give 6-8-10, 9-12-15, and 12-16-20" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Scaling 3-4-5 by 2, 3, and 4 gives 6-8-10, 9-12-15, and 12-16-20. Every scaled set is still a valid Pythagorean triple.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

