<!-- Section 14: sec-worked -->
<section id="sec-worked" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Worked Examples Using the Calculator</h2>
  <p>Eight examples covering both modes: four checks and four generations.</p>
  <h3>Example 1: Check 3, 4, 5</h3>
  <p>Enter <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} /> in check mode. Square the legs and add: <span set:html={rich('[[3^2+4^2=9+16=25]]')} />. Square the hypotenuse: <span set:html={rich('[[5^2=25]]')} />. Since <span set:html={rich('[[25=25]]')} />, the result is a valid triple. It is primitive, because <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} /> share no common factor.</p>
  <h3>Example 2: Check 5, 12, 13</h3>
  <p>Enter <span set:html={rich('[[a=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=12]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c=13]]')} />. The legs give <span set:html={rich('[[5^2+12^2=25+144=169]]')} />, and the hypotenuse gives <span set:html={rich('[[13^2=169]]')} />. Since <span set:html={rich('[[169=169]]')} />, the result is a valid triple. It is primitive, because <span set:html={rich('[[\\gcd(5,12,13)=1]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Example 3: Check 6, 8, 10</h3>
  <p>Enter <span set:html={rich('[[a=6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=8]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} />. The legs give <span set:html={rich('[[6^2+8^2=36+64=100]]')} />, and the hypotenuse gives <span set:html={rich('[[10^2=100]]')} />. Since <span set:html={rich('[[100=100]]')} />, the result is a valid triple. It is non-primitive, because all three sides share the factor <span set:html={rich('[[2]]')} />; dividing by <span set:html={rich('[[2]]')} /> returns the primitive triple <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 4, 5)]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Example 4: Check 7, 24, 25</h3>
  <p>Enter <span set:html={rich('[[a=7]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=24]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c=25]]')} />. The legs give <span set:html={rich('[[7^2+24^2=49+576=625]]')} />, and the hypotenuse gives <span set:html={rich('[[25^2=625]]')} />. Since <span set:html={rich('[[625=625]]')} />, the result is a valid triple. It is primitive, because <span set:html={rich('[[7]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[24]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[25]]')} /> share no common factor.</p>
  <h3>Example 5: Generate using m = 2, n = 1</h3>
  <p>Enter <span set:html={rich('[[m=2]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n=1]]')} /> in generate mode. The first leg is <span set:html={rich('[[a=2^2-1^2=3]]')} />, the second leg is <span set:html={rich('[[b=2(2)(1)=4]]')} />, and the hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[c=2^2+1^2=5]]')} />. The result is <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 4, 5)]]')} />, a primitive triple.</p>
  <h3>Example 6: Generate using m = 3, n = 2</h3>
  <p>Enter <span set:html={rich('[[m=3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n=2]]')} />. The first leg is <span set:html={rich('[[a=3^2-2^2=9-4=5]]')} />, the second leg is <span set:html={rich('[[b=2(3)(2)=12]]')} />, and the hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[c=3^2+2^2=13]]')} />. The result is <span set:html={rich('[[(5, 12, 13)]]')} />, a primitive triple, because <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[2]]')} /> are coprime with opposite parity.</p>
  <h3>Example 7: Generate using m = 4, n = 1</h3>
  <p>Enter <span set:html={rich('[[m=4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n=1]]')} />. The formulas give <span set:html={rich('[[a=4^2-1^2=15]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=2(4)(1)=8]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c=4^2+1^2=17]]')} />. Listed with the legs in ascending order, the result is <span set:html={rich('[[(8, 15, 17)]]')} />. It is primitive, because <span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} /> are coprime with opposite parity.</p>
  <h3>Example 8: Generate using m = 3, n = 1</h3>
  <p>Enter <span set:html={rich('[[m=3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n=1]]')} />. The formulas give <span set:html={rich('[[a=3^2-1^2=8]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=2(3)(1)=6]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c=3^2+1^2=10]]')} />. Listed with the legs in ascending order, the result is <span set:html={rich('[[(6, 8, 10)]]')} />. This triple is non-primitive: <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} /> are coprime, but both are odd, so the opposite-parity condition fails and every side comes out even. Dividing by <span set:html={rich('[[2]]')} /> returns the primitive triple <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 4, 5)]]')} />.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: sec-infinite -->
<section id="sec-infinite" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Are There Infinitely Many Pythagorean Triples?</h2>
  <p>Euclid's formula never runs out of inputs, so it never runs out of triples.</p>
  <p>Euclid's formula accepts any pair of positive integers <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} /> with <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> greater than <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} />, and there are infinitely many such pairs. Each new pair produces a triple, and different pairs generally produce different triples. The pairs <span set:html={rich('[[(2, 1)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 2)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[(4, 1)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[(4, 3)]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[(5, 2)]]')} /> give <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 4, 5)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[(5, 12, 13)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[(8, 15, 17)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[(7, 24, 25)]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[(20, 21, 29)]]')} /> respectively, and the list keeps going: <span set:html={rich('[[(5, 4)]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[(9, 40, 41)]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[(6, 1)]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[(12, 35, 37)]]')} />. Larger values of <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> keep producing larger triples without end.</p>
  <p>Integer multiples add even more. Every primitive triple generates a whole family of scaled copies: <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 4, 5)]]')} /> alone gives <span set:html={rich('[[(6, 8, 10)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[(9, 12, 15)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[(12, 16, 20)]]')} />, and so on forever. Between the endless parameter pairs and the endless multiples, the supply of Pythagorean triples is infinite. Note the careful wording: not every parameter pair produces a primitive triple, but every pair produces a valid triple, and the primitive ones are infinite too.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 16: sec-recognize -->
<section id="sec-recognize" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Recognize a Pythagorean Triple Without Guessing</h2>
  <p>A few habits make familiar triples jump out before you calculate.</p>
  <p>Start by treating the largest value as the hypotenuse and comparing the squared values, exactly as the checking steps describe. With practice, the common triples become familiar: <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[12]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[13]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[8]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[15]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[17]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[7]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[24]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[25]]')} /> appear so often in textbooks and exams that recognizing them saves real time.</p>
  <p>Look for common factors next. If all three numbers are even, divide by <span set:html={rich('[[2]]')} /> and see what remains. The set <span set:html={rich('[[6]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[8]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[10]]')} /> is instantly recognizable as twice <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} /> once the factor <span set:html={rich('[[2]]')} /> is spotted. Reducing a non-primitive triple to its primitive form often reveals a familiar pattern hiding behind larger numbers.</p>
  <p>When you need a new triple rather than a check, Euclid's formula is the reliable route: pick small values of <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} /> and generate. Recognition is a helpful shortcut, but exact verification is always the final word. A triple that looks familiar still deserves the square-and-compare test before you rely on it.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 17: sec-ordinary -->
<section id="sec-ordinary" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Pythagorean Triples and Ordinary Right Triangles</h2>
  <p>Every triple is a right triangle, but most right triangles are not triples.</p>
  <p>A right triangle can have integer side lengths, decimal side lengths, or irrational side lengths. Only the first category can form a Pythagorean triple. The triple <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 4, 5)]]')} /> is an integer right triangle, and it qualifies. A triangle with legs <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} /> and hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> is a genuine right triangle, since <span set:html={rich('[[1+1=2]]')} />, but its hypotenuse is irrational, so it is not a triple. A triangle with sides <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[5.1]]')} /> fails the equation, since <span set:html={rich('[[9+16=25]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[5.1^2=26.01]]')} />, so it is not even a right triangle.</p>
  <p>The distinction matters in practice. Builders use <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} /> because whole numbers are easy to measure with a tape. A triangle with sides <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[5.1]]')} /> looks almost right but is off by a visible angle, and no whole-number check would pass it. The Pythagorean theorem applies to all right triangles, whatever their side lengths. The term Pythagorean triple refers only to the positive integer solutions, and that narrow meaning is what makes triples special.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 18: sec-mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Using the Pythagorean Triples Calculator</h2>
  <p>Fifteen mistakes, each with the fix beside it.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Entering <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> as a smaller side: the hypotenuse must be the largest value, so always assign the biggest number to <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> before checking.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to square the values: the theorem compares squares, not the side lengths themselves, so square <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> first.</li>
    <li>Adding the sides instead of their squares: <span set:html={rich('[[a+b]]')} /> is the wrong sum; the check needs <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2]]')} /> against <span set:html={rich('[[c^2]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Using <span set:html={rich('[[a+b=c]]')} /> instead of <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} />: the plain sum of two sides never equals the hypotenuse in a right triangle.</li>
    <li>Confusing the hypotenuse with a leg: the hypotenuse sits opposite the right angle and is always the longest side.</li>
    <li>Using decimal values and expecting an integer triple: decimals can form a right triangle, but only positive integers form a Pythagorean triple.</li>
    <li>Entering zero or negative side lengths: side lengths must be positive, and zero or negative inputs are rejected.</li>
    <li>Using <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> equal to <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} />: the first leg becomes <span set:html={rich('[[m^2-n^2=0]]')} />, which is not a triangle side.</li>
    <li>Using <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> smaller than <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} />: the first leg becomes negative, which is impossible for a side length.</li>
    <li>Entering decimal <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} /> values: Euclid's formula needs positive integer parameters to produce integer sides.</li>
    <li>Assuming every generated triple is primitive: generation only guarantees a valid triple, and primitiveness needs coprime parameters with opposite parity.</li>
    <li>Assuming all primitive triples are multiples of <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[12]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[13]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[8]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[15]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[17]]')} /> are independent primitive triples, not multiples of <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} />-<span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Forgetting the opposite-parity condition: two odd parameters produce an even, non-primitive triple, as <span set:html={rich('[[m=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[n=1]]')} /> shows.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to check common factors: a triple such as <span set:html={rich('[[(12, 16, 20)]]')} /> looks new until the shared factor <span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} /> reveals it as a scaled <span set:html={rich('[[(3, 4, 5)]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Confusing a valid right triangle with an integer Pythagorean triple: a triangle with irrational or decimal sides can satisfy the theorem without being a triple.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

